 "title": "Indexes: skip the full table scan",
 "kind": "structure",
 "intro": "Finding one name among a million rows. Checking every row would take forever, so what does an index do instead?",
 "predict": {
  "question": "With an index, about how many steps to find one name among 1,000,000 rows?",
  "options": [
   "About 20",
   "About 1,000",
   "About 500,000"
  ],
  "answer": 0,
  "reveal": "An index is a sorted tree. Each step cuts the search space in half, so a million rows need only about 20 steps."
 },
 "takeaway": "Without an index the database reads every row. A sorted index lets it jump straight to the match, at the cost of extra space and slower writes.",
